- Hereditary spastic paraplegia (HSP) — often normal MRI, pure upper motor neuron signs;  
- Vitamin B12 deficiency or copper deficiency — subacute combined degeneration can present with spastic paraparesis and bladder dysfunction, sometimes with minimal sensory complaints early on;  
- Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (CIDP) — though typically sensory-motor, “motor-predominant” variants exist;  
- Paraneoplastic or autoimmune myelopathy (e.g., anti-Hu, anti-CV2) — but no cancer history;  
- HTLV-1–associated myelopathy (HAM/TSP) — especially given urinary incontinence predating gait decline, though travel history was negative; however, HTLV-1 can be endemic in non-travel populations via blood transfusion or vertical transmission.  

Serum B12, copper, and HTLV-1 serology are highly specific, low-risk, and readily available tests that can rapidly confirm or exclude treatable or epidemiologically relevant causes. Among these, HTLV-1 testing is particularly important: HAM/TSP classically presents with insidious-onset spastic paraparesis and urinary incontinence — often years before gait impairment — and MRI may be normal early on.

[TEST] Serum HTLV-1 antibody test.

